The conviction "there is no {{pradhāna|primordial nature}}" is [the inverted contentment called] "ajambhikā"; "there is no great principle" is [the inversion of] "salila"; and "āyācchā" [is inverted] in [the denial] "there is no I-maker";
or "there is no unseen [merit]"—[the denial that] the pentad of subtle elements is the cause of the [gross] elements. "All this is just brahman; being brahman alone, one attains brahman"—as is the view of the Vedāntins, so [erroneously, here is held to be] such an opinion of [these false] Sāṃkhyas.
Engagement in acquiring objects [is the inverted external contentment "asutā"]; engagement in safeguarding [them is] "sutarā"; engagement in [amassing] wealth by one who does not see the fault of acquisition [is] "asunetrā"—so it is proclaimed by the Sāṃkhyas.

and desisting from [pursuit of] wealth because of the fault of violence and the rest—the ninth contentment, called "samaskarī" [uttamāmbhasikā], is the highest of these, [O disciple].
The knowledge that arises [even] from afar [i.e. by reasoning] is the first, named "tārā"; knowledge of beings [arising] by mere [hearing of] words is held to be "sutārā";
knowledge [arising] through study, which carries one across, is the third; from the removal of internal (adhyātmika) suffering it is called "pramodā";
from the removal of elemental (ādhibhautika) affliction the attainment "pramuditā" is named; from the removal of divine (ādhidaivika) affliction, knowledge arises for men—
the sixth attainment, of the virtuous, is called "pramodamānā"; from the cessation of [the fault of] affectionate association, the seventh is remembered as "ramyakā";

He sits somewhere upon the road, a victim waiting for the light.
The world will watch when from my heart arises the reckoning of speech;
My silence is no silence, but a tomb for the word of longing's reach.
The river's wave began to say, "My glory comes from journeying,"
The pearl replied, "To stay inside is the source of my honor's sheen."
A soul whose nature is complete, no training can make it more true;
The stream-side cypress's reflection could not turn green, though in the view.
I saw no heart where there was not a dream of some desire held true;
My God, what is this world of Yours? A gallery of aspiration's view.
At death it was revealed our life was just a spell of grasping need;
What we had thought a body of clay was dust of the lane of our desire's creed.
If nothing in this world is veiled, why am I searching head to toe?

The same pale hue as moonlit night it wears;
As in the Splendor of Sinai, the White Hand of Moses,
As in the garden's fragrant wave, the scent a bud discloses—
So is my heart within the torrent of Your love;
If You are the assembly, I'm the life that makes it move.
You are the lightning-flash of Beauty; I, Love's culmination,
If You are dawn, my tears become Your dew's creation.
If I'm the eve of exile, You become my twilight glow;
Within my heart, the chaos of Your scattered tresses flow.
From Your mere image, my astonishment is born;
Your Beauty is perfected, and my Love is perfect form.
For this, my garden of verse, You are the breeze of spring;
To my restless imagination, You gave its deep repose.
Since first Your love came to inhabit this my breast,
New splendors in my mirror have been made manifest.
